    "title": "Sharp lower bound for the Hausdorff dimension of Cantor-series exact approximation sets via a mass distribution principle",
    "description": "For a Cantor base sequence q with q_k >= 2, write Q_n = q_0...q_{n-1} and let omega_n(x) be the level-n truncation of the Cantor series of x in [0,1). For monotone psi with sum_n psi(n)*Q_n < infinity and log q_n / log Q_n -> 0, consider the exact approximation set E_c(psi) = {x : x - omega_n(x) < psi(n)/Q_n infinitely often}. The convergence half already gives that E_c(psi) is Lebesgue-null. The proposed program is to establish the matching lower bound dim_H E_c(psi) >= 2/lambda, where lambda = liminf_n (-log psi(n))/log Q_n, by constructing a Cantor-like subset supporting a probability measure mu with mu(B(x,r)) <= r^{2/lambda - epsilon} and applying the mass distribution (Frostman) principle. The key technical step is a two-scale self-similar construction that places, in each surviving cylinder, exactly the sub-cylinders whose tails realize order psi and no better, so that the exponent 2/lambda emerges from the product of a horizontal covering count and a vertical exactness constraint.",
